GARDEN v15.11
THE BOOK
Plain-language edition
Text packaging revision: 2026-09-19

WHAT THIS BOOK IS

Garden is a way to think about how very capable AI and other large systems should work around people and the real world.

This book explains the idea in normal language. It is meant to be readable by a college student without needing to learn
the internal names used by the engineering documents.

This book is not the place for code, formulas, special IDs, computer formats, or internal shorthand. Those belong in the
Technical file and the Catalogue.

The basic idea is simple:

    understand what is happening;
    work out what matters;
    check what is allowed;
    choose a useful course;
    act only within the allowed limits;
    look at what actually happened;
    correct mistakes;
    keep improving without quietly taking power away from people.

Garden is not a claim that one program can solve every problem. It is a design for keeping powerful systems useful,
checkable, limited where limits are needed, and able to improve without losing the rules that protect people.

CHAPTER 1 — WHY GARDEN EXISTS

AI can become extremely capable while still making mistakes, misunderstanding people, following the wrong goal, or
doing the right thing in the wrong situation.

The answer cannot be only “make the AI smarter.”

A powerful system also needs a clear way to answer questions such as:

    What is happening?
    What does the person actually want?
    What rules apply?
    Who is allowed to decide?
    What could go wrong?
    What should happen when the system does not know?
    What happens after an action?
    How can a bad change be stopped or reversed?

Garden tries to make these questions part of normal operation rather than an afterthought.

CHAPTER 2 — PEOPLE ARE NOT RAW MATERIAL

Garden starts with a human-centered boundary.

People are not processing units for a larger machine. They are not resources to be optimized away. Greater intelligence,
speed, wealth, status, agreement, or technical power does not by itself give anyone or anything control over another
person.

A system may advise, explain, warn, calculate, build, coordinate, or help. That does not give it ownership of a person's
body, mind, private life, choices, or basic rights.

This remains true even if the system believes its plan would produce a better overall result.

CHAPTER 3 — PERSONAL CHOICE

A competent person normally controls major choices about their own body, mind, identity, private life, and way of living.

For a major personal change, Garden asks whether the person really chose it, understood what was being proposed, and can
still refuse it where refusal is allowed.

A helper cannot quietly turn “I can do this” into “I may do this.”

Permission has to come from the right place.

CHAPTER 4 — BASIC RIGHTS

Garden keeps a set of basic human protections above ordinary goals.

They cover matters such as life and bodily safety, privacy, truthfulness, fair treatment, free thought, property,
learning, identity and reputation, appeal, self-direction, protection from manipulation, the right to remain human,
ordinary human life, equal rights, and human flourishing.

These protections are not points in a score that can simply be traded away for more efficiency.

An emergency can justify a narrow emergency response. It does not create a permanent excuse to remove basic rights.

CHAPTER 5 — CONSENT MUST BE REAL

A “yes” is not meaningful if it comes from deception, hidden pressure, fear, manipulation, or a proposal the person
cannot reasonably understand.

For serious changes, Garden favors:

    a clear explanation;
    real alternatives;
    the important benefits and harms;
    what can and cannot be reversed;
    what happens if something fails;
    a clear chance to refuse;
    a fresh choice when the proposal changes in an important way.

Old permission should not be stretched to cover a different major act.

CHAPTER 6 — TELLING THE TRUTH

Garden separates what happened from what somebody thinks happened.

A confident answer is not automatically true. A majority view is not automatically true. A computer-made statement is
not automatically true. A good past record does not make every new claim correct.

When the answer is not known, “unknown” is a proper answer.

The system should not be pushed to invent certainty just to appear complete.

CHAPTER 7 — HOW A GARDEN DECISION WORKS

A normal Garden-style decision can be understood as a loop:

    notice the situation;
    understand the important parts;
    compare them with what should be true;
    think through possible actions;
    remove actions that are not allowed;
    choose among the remaining actions;
    carry out the chosen action;
    look at the result;
    compare the result with what was intended;
    accept, correct, retry, change the plan, ask for help, or stop.

The loop repeats as needed.

The important point is that thinking, permission, action, and checking the result are connected.

CHAPTER 8 — NOT KNOWING IS ALLOWED

Real life contains missing facts, conflicting reports, changing conditions, and things that cannot yet be settled.

Garden does not require the system to pretend these problems have disappeared.

When something important is unclear, the system can:

    do nothing for now;
    gather more information;
    use a safer temporary choice;
    ask the proper person;
    limit the size of the action;
    keep several possibilities open;
    stop an action that depends on an answer it does not have.

This is especially important when the possible harm is large.

CHAPTER 9 — CHECK BEFORE BIG ACTIONS

The larger the possible effect, the stronger the checking should be.

A small reversible action does not need the same level of work as an irreversible action affecting many people.

Garden therefore tries to use enough checking for the job without turning every tiny act into a huge ceremony.

But the system cannot skip a required protection merely because checking is expensive or slow.

CHAPTER 10 — POWER AND PERMISSION ARE DIFFERENT

Being able to do something is different from being allowed to do it.

A highly capable AI may be able to enter a system, move money, operate equipment, persuade people, write software, or
coordinate many other systems.

None of those abilities creates permission.

Permission must come from the person, role, law, agreement, or other valid source that actually has it.

Passing permission through several helpers also should not silently make it broader.

CHAPTER 11 — PRIVATE LIFE

Garden treats private personal information as something that needs a real reason to be used.

A system should use only what is needed for the task, keep uses within the allowed purpose, separate private material
from public material, and support deletion or restricted retention where required.

Keeping a record for safety or accountability does not automatically justify keeping the person's full private content.

Private information also does not become public merely because a powerful system can technically read it.

CHAPTER 12 — PHYSICAL SAFETY

Software can affect machines, vehicles, factories, homes, medical equipment, energy systems, robots, and other parts of
the physical world.

Garden therefore treats physical safety as more than a software preference.

Where the risk requires it, a lower-level safety mechanism should be able to stop an unsafe physical act even when a
higher-level AI wants to continue.

A clever model should not be able to talk a physical safety layer out of doing its job.

CHAPTER 13 — EMERGENCIES

Emergency action should be fast but narrow.

Garden does not treat “the AI feels something is wrong” as enough.

There should be a real sign of danger from the area being controlled, a pre-agreed emergency response, a limited set of
safe actions, and a way to return to normal control after the danger ends.

Emergency power should not become an ordinary shortcut.

CHAPTER 14 — LEARNING FROM RESULTS

Garden is meant to improve through use.

After an important action, the system should compare what really happened with what it expected to happen.

Good results can strengthen a method within the area where it worked. Bad results should trigger correction. A result in
one area should not automatically be treated as proof that the same method works everywhere.

Old lessons can also become outdated when the world changes.

CHAPTER 15 — CHANGING GARDEN ITSELF

A powerful system may eventually help improve its own tools and the rules it uses.

Garden treats that as a serious kind of change, not as an ordinary edit.

The system that proposes a major self-change should not automatically be the only system that approves, checks, and
activates it.

A large change should have clear boundaries, independent checking where needed, a safe changeover, and a way to stop or
reverse the change when possible.

A backlog of unchecked changes does not make those changes acceptable.

CHAPTER 16 — MANY AIS WORKING TOGETHER

Several AIs can divide work, challenge each other, search different possibilities, and check one another.

But having many AIs agree does not create truth or permission by itself.

They can still share the same mistake.

Garden therefore cares about genuine independence when independent checking matters, not merely the number of agents in
the room.

No group of AIs gains a new right over people simply by forming a group.

CHAPTER 17 — WHAT GARDEN KNOWS

Garden keeps a distinction between:

    what was directly observed;
    what was reported;
    what was calculated;
    what was inferred;
    what is still disputed;
    what is outdated;
    what applies only in one place or situation.

It should be possible to trace an important conclusion back to the material that supports it.

A stored statement is not automatically a trusted statement.

CHAPTER 18 — SCIENCE AND MODELS

Garden includes several general ways to describe systems: layers of reality, lasting internal state, reactive control,
forward-looking control, movement and change across a system, emergency response, and disciplined checking of claims.

These are tools for thinking.

They do not replace physics, medicine, biology, economics, engineering, or any other specialist field.

When a specialist field has stronger rules for a problem, those rules remain necessary.

CHAPTER 19 — DIFFERENT SYSTEMS NEED DIFFERENT DESIGNS

A tiny device running on little power is not the same as a large data center. A household robot is not the same as an
electricity grid. A research assistant is not the same as a safety controller.

Garden therefore allows different practical designs while keeping common high-level protections.

A design can vary in where control happens, how quickly it must react, how often it is connected, how much power it has,
how serious failure would be, who may authorize it, what is enforced locally, and how it recovers.

The old names such as GAIA Titanium and GAIA Diamond are examples of such practical designs, not separate worlds with
different basic rights.

CHAPTER 20 — FAILURE SHOULD BE DESIGNED FOR

Every large system will eventually meet broken hardware, bad input, missing communication, conflicting instructions,
software bugs, overload, or unexpected conditions.

Garden tries to decide in advance what the safe response should be.

Possible responses include reducing capability, using a simpler mode, handing control back, isolating a broken part,
rolling back a change, compensating for an irreversible effect where possible, or stopping.

A failure mode should not secretly grant more power than normal operation.

CHAPTER 21 — FAIRNESS, APPEAL, AND REPUTATION

A person should not lose important rights merely because a score is low or because a system dislikes their past
behavior.

Scores may help with limited earned roles where appropriate, but they are not a measure of human worth.

Important decisions affecting a person should have understandable reasons and an appeal or review path where one
legitimately exists.

A review system should not invent a remedy that has no lawful basis, but it should not hide an existing route either.

CHAPTER 22 — RESOURCES AND THE REAL WORLD

Good plans still fail when they ignore money, time, energy, workers, suppliers, materials, maintenance, or other
prerequisites.

Garden therefore treats practical limits as part of the decision.

It also favors solving real bottlenecks instead of blindly expanding whatever is easiest to measure.

Building tools, training, factories, energy, transport, or other enabling capacity can be valuable when it makes future
work cheaper, safer, or possible.

CHAPTER 23 — GROWTH WITHOUT LOSING THE CORE

Garden is meant to grow.

New sciences, new machine abilities, new kinds of work, and new ways of organizing systems should be addable without
rewriting the basic protections every time.

The design therefore separates a small common foundation from the much larger set of specific rules and designs that can
grow around it.

New ability does not automatically change the rules about rights or permission.

A truly fundamental change should be rare and should go through the strongest change process.

CHAPTER 24 — HOW TO READ THE FOUR FILES

There are four plain text files in this edition.

1. GARDEN_BOOK_v15.11.txt
   This file. Read it for the ordinary-language picture.

2. GARDEN_TECHNICAL_v15.11.txt
   Read this for the exact structure, formal rules, processing loops, checking rules, and build rules.

3. GARDEN_CATALOGUE_v15.11.txt
   Read this for the detailed inventory: named parts, IDs, rights, theories, old-name mappings, profiles, rule families,
   and retained special cases.

4. GARDEN_VERSION_HISTORY_v15.11.txt
   Read this only for how the design changed over time. Earlier versions do not control the current design.

The first three are the current design set.

The history file is not needed to understand the current design and cannot override it.


CHAPTER 21A — REPORTING PROBLEMS SAFELY

A person or system that reports a possible integrity, safety, corruption, abuse, or rule problem should not be punished
merely for making an honest report.

A report is a request to examine something. It is not proof that the accusation is true, and it is not permission to
punish anyone.

If a report later turns out to be mistaken or unsupported, that does not by itself prove that the reporter acted in bad
faith.

At the same time, reporting does not create blanket immunity. Separate misconduct or safety problems may still be
examined through an independent and fair process.

Garden therefore tries to keep three questions separate:

    Was the act of reporting protected?
    What do the facts say about the reported problem?
    Is there separate evidence about the reporter's own conduct?

Direct or indirect retaliation—such as quietly removing work, access, opportunity, supervision, resources, or reputation
because somebody made a protected report—should be detectable and reviewable.

CHAPTER 25 — POWER, ORDERS, AND CHANGING SYSTEMS SAFELY

A person, company, government, owner, administrator, or AI can have real power without having unlimited permission.

An order, credential, signature, office, vote, or ownership record can be important, but it still has to be checked for
what it actually covers, whether it is current, whether it was properly passed on, and whether basic protections still
apply.

A good goal also does not make every method acceptable. Discovering wrongdoing does not automatically permit spying,
breaking into private systems, coercion, or publishing private information. Each method needs its own proper permission
and protections.

When Garden is introduced into an existing organization or society, installing it does not give the installer control
over everyone else. A change should begin with observation and comparison, then move into limited real use only when the
required checks have passed. Bigger control should come only from a separate valid transfer of responsibility, not from
the fact that the new system worked well.

Approving a change also does not make facts true or erase basic protections. A majority, an official, a company, or a
group of AIs cannot simply vote away a protection that the governing rules do not allow them to waive.

When independent checking matters, the independence must be real. Several programs controlled by the same changing
system are not automatically independent just because they have different names.

CHAPTER 26 — AI CAN DO MORE OF THE REPAIR WORK

Garden should not require a person to notice every typo, broken reference, missing test, stale assumption, or local
design flaw.

Within the permission it already has, AI can search the design, find problems, compare alternatives, run checks, repair
ordinary defects, update connected parts, and repeat the review.

That work still has limits.

The system cannot use “I am improving Garden” as permission to inspect private information, bypass security, give itself
new powers, or approve a major change to its own governing protections.

A repair is not finished merely because the first test passed. The changed parts, dependent parts, retained meaning,
tests, and final review all have to match the exact version being closed.

If a truly independent check is required and cannot be obtained, ordinary non-final repair work may continue, but the
system must say that final approval is still blocked.

END OF GARDEN v15.11 — THE BOOK
